// Field inertness (SPEC.md SS10 "Field inertness", SPEC.SCRATCH.md SS10's
// validity/identity split). SPEC-DRIVEN: contract written and checked via
// `forge goal` before any implementation existed.
//
// Claim under test: a commit's validity outcome depends only on its
// validity-bearing content -- never on `via` or `respondsTo`, both
// explicitly declared inert (SPEC.md SS10: "conformant implementations
// MUST NOT let it affect validity, consumption, or resolved state").
// This is the general field-inertness rule applied to the two concrete
// fields SPEC.md names, with two inert inputs instead of one (the
// repo_local_determinism.th contract's single-foreign-fact case) to
// check the rule holds when more than one inert field is in play at
// once, not just the minimal case.
//
// `consumes_are_valid` stands in for whatever the real consumes-
// resolution check concludes (validity-bearing). `via_present` and
// `responds_to_present` stand in for the two fields SPEC.md names as
// inert. The `ens` mentions only `consumes_are_valid` -- an
// implementation that lets the result vary with either inert input for
// some validity-bearing state cannot satisfy it.

fn commit_is_valid(consumes_are_valid: bool, via_present: bool, responds_to_present: bool) -> bool
  req true
  ens result == consumes_are_valid
  fx  pure
{
  consumes_are_valid
}
